# Nexus @VERSION@ — Windows x64

Nexus v1 · Network Intelligence Platform · designed and developed by Hasan Güler

| File | SHA-256 |
|---|---|
| `@FILE@` | `@SHA256@` |

Signature: @SIGNED@.

## Install

Run `@FILE@` and accept the Windows administrator prompt. Setup checks the
computer, installs Nexus as a Windows service with its own PostgreSQL @PGVERSION@
database, verifies that it works and opens it in the browser, where you create
the administrator account.

Nothing else needs to be installed: no Docker, WSL, PostgreSQL, Python or
Node.js, and no internet access is required. Supported: Windows 10 1809 or
newer, Windows 11, Windows Server 2019 or newer, x64.

Unattended installation:

```
@FILE@ /S [/PORT=8080] [/LAN=1] [/DEMO=1] [/NOSTART] [/NODESKTOP] [/NOBROWSER] [/LOG=C:\path\install.log] [/D=C:\Program Files\Nexus]
```

Exit codes: 0 success · 2 installation failed · 3 upgrade failed, previous version restored.

## Where things are

| What | Location |
|---|---|
| Program | `C:\Program Files\Nexus\app\@VERSION@\` |
| Configuration | `C:\ProgramData\Nexus\config\nexus.env` |
| Encryption key, database password | `C:\ProgramData\Nexus\secrets\` (administrators and the service only) |
| Database | `C:\ProgramData\Nexus\data\db\` |
| Logs | `C:\ProgramData\Nexus\logs\` (`install.log`, `nexus.log`, `postgres-*.log`) |
| Backups | `C:\ProgramData\Nexus\backups\` (daily and before every upgrade) |

The web interface listens on `http://localhost:<port>/` only, unless
"Allow access from other computers" was chosen; then a Windows Firewall rule
allows the port on domain and private networks (never public networks).

## Upgrade, uninstall, backup

* Running a newer installer upgrades in place: a backup is written first, the
  new version is started and verified, and the previous version is restored
  automatically if it does not become healthy.
* Uninstall from Settings → Apps. Data is kept unless you tick
  "Also delete all Nexus data" (silent: `"Uninstall Nexus.exe" /S /PURGEDATA=YES`).
* Settings → Diagnostics & backup in the web interface exports backups and
  redacted diagnostic bundles. Keep the file `secrets\master.key` (or export a
  backup with the key protected by a passphrase): stored device credentials
  can only be decrypted with it.

See `docs/windows.md` in the source repository for details.
